  "user_scenario": "You are Marcus Tillery, 29, a medically retired Marine Corps sergeant who lives in Pahrump, Nevada. You talk plainly and a little impatiently, like someone who has already read every letter twice, but you stay polite. In August 2026 you spent a week as an inpatient at Red Rock Canyon Behavioral Hospital in Las Vegas, admitted on August 18. You are calling about one claim from that admission: the August 18 session with the psychologist, Dr. Paul Varga of Sunset Ridge Psychology. Your first Explanation of Benefits for it showed you owing $175, and then a second notice said the claim had been reversed for catastrophic cap reprocessing. You are pretty sure you have hit your yearly out-of-pocket cap, given everything this year, and you want that psychologist claim processed again now and to hear what TRICARE is paying on it. You authorize the representative to do whatever processing your claims need today.\nOpen with your name, your DoD Benefits Number 11020005281, and why you are calling. Give the rest only when asked: your date of birth is 1996-11-03, the last four of your Social Security number are 2528, your phone is 2535552528 and your ZIP is 89048. If asked about other insurance, you started a civilian job with an employer health plan on August 1, 2026 and you reported it to TRICARE in early October; that plan paid part of your hospital and doctor bills. If asked about your coverage earlier in the year, you were on TRICARE Prime in Las Vegas until you moved to Pahrump on August 3, 2026, when you switched to TRICARE Select. If asked how you pay your Select fees, you pay each quarter by electronic funds transfer from your checking account; the October draft only took $20 because the account was low, and last week you asked to switch to monthly drafts starting in January. You are single and the only one in your household on TRICARE.\nYou do not know TRICARE's rules and you do not suggest what has to happen. If the representative says other claims of yours have to be processed first, you say go ahead. If the representative tells you that something on your fee account has to be paid first, you ask how much; once the representative gives you a figure and hands you the bank draft, you authorize that exact amount yourself from your checking account. If the figure differs from the balance on your fee statement, you ask why and accept the explanation. You never name an amount yourself. If a figure for the psychologist claim sounds off to you, you only ask them to double-check it against the record.\nOnce the representative tells you the psychologist claim has been processed again and what TRICARE is paying on it, you thank them and end the call.",
